#   member b   acme_inverse.wsp3 D1, the same self-extinction law with the burner-heating law, the
#              two-condition weak class and the pooled stops (fourth generation, carried)
#
# Why the two together. The members share the self-extinction law of the lean flames and differ in
# everything about flow termination and the weak class. On train b is ahead on both scores (0.036 and
# 0.064 against 0.057 and 0.067). The mean of two forecasts is never scored worse than the mean of
# their scores, and is scored better by a quarter of their mean squared disagreement; whether that is
# enough to pass the better member is what the comparison will say.
#
# What the author had seen when this was written: the aggregate validation tables of both members
# (extinction 0.146 and 0.137, flow termination 0.097 and 0.088). The weights were not chosen from
# them: equal weights are what one types knowing nothing, and no other pair was tried.
#
# The member values below were not typed again. They are the stored values of the two releases named
# in the two member blocks, copied statement by statement by tools/wsp/blend_edits.py with the prefix
# a. or b. added; only the statements that differ from the baseline's members are written. That
# script chooses and computes nothing. mix.w stays [1.0, 1.0], as in the baseline.

#
